{"step_index":48,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:56:13Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"total domination number\" \"roman domination number\"","toolAction":"Searching literature on total vs roman domination","toolSummary":"Search web for total vs roman domination"}}]}
{"step_index":49,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:56:16Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:56:16+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:56:22+02:00\nThe search for \"\"total domination number\" \"roman domination number\"\" returned the following summary:\nIn graph theory, both **total domination number** and **roman domination number** are parameters related to the concept of dominating sets, representing different strategies for covering or \"defending\" a graph[1][2][3].\n\n### Total Domination Number\nThe **total domination number**, denoted by $\\gamma_t(G)$, is defined as the minimum cardinality (number of vertices) of a **total dominating set**[4][5].\n*   **Definition:** A set $S$ of vertices in a graph $G = (V, E)$ is a total dominating set if every vertex in $V$ (including those in $S$) is adjacent to at least one vertex in $S$[5][6].\n*   **Key Constraint:** Unlike standard domination, where vertices in the set can dominate themselves, total domination requires every vertex to have a *distinct* neighbor in the set[5]. Consequently, graphs with isolated vertices do not have a total dominating set[5][7].\n\n### Roman Domination Number\nThe **roman domination number**, denoted by $\\gamma_R(G)$, is a weighted variation of the domination problem[2][8].\n*   **Definition:** It is the minimum total weight of a **Roman dominating function** $f: V \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$[2][8].\n*   **Function Rule:** The function must satisfy the condition that every vertex $v$ with $f(v) = 0$ is adjacent to at least one vertex $u$ with $f(u) = 2$[8][9]. \n*   **Conceptual Origin:** The concept is famously motivated by a *Scientific American* article, \"Defend the Roman Empire!\"[8]. It models a scenario where locations (vertices) are either:\n    *   Unsecured ($f(v)=0$)[8]\n    *   Secured by one legion ($f(v)=1$)[8]\n    *   Secured by two legions ($f(v)=2$), allowing one legion to be sent to an adjacent unsecured location without leaving the original vertex unprotected[8].\n\n### Summary of Differences\n| Feature | Total Domination | Roman Domination |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Mathematical Basis** | Cardinality of a vertex set[5] | Total weight of a function labeling[2] |\n| **Labels/Values** | Binary (In set / Not in set) | Discrete $\\{0, 1, 2\\}$[8] |\n| **Primary Goal** | Every vertex must have a neighbor in $S$[5] | Every $0$-vertex must be adjacent to a $2$-vertex[8] |\n\nResearch also explores hybrid variants, such as **total Roman domination**, which combines the requirements of both concepts[10][11].\n\nSources:\n[1] [emergentmind.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFaucFiyddctJSxa7K7v1t8GPWtAO4w1NnJHAoTyipMq8QlXrreOiqGzQ-S7k9nQl8pwpybDwzR3-oxVxTwqmnAQPMDhQZe2MJ_CseyNPZs-H9o7_JNyG5PsSnS1iE-Lt1dc69gYKSCNBggFou5npQZew==)\n[2] [emergentmind.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH8foRoKPrUiQ6LrVoOtNKa10_IiecWzpiPDY17wVPwhlnTb3KS0HeMNxRjbbIZH8beONZ4zlgB7Vdp9q-cF0Q9E1J5k10tv16kQyAwTH_scjaBt56hb2_dCI3jEWMUpWWTwB0DC8ypS3Sw1BfrZK7QbA==)\n[3] [ijsdr.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF5CbYnp5i63W-dwZ39F8b-p5vxUIECIzCwHOODIhIFy-NGnBEki2cEHHzn2VbQzmmBZDVHpdVhTyLleUkRGpObuJ7FcYFN7DKXM6q8HIUu77ZwLuw19XmJ8ZhueL0gpq7OelQ=)\n[4] [wolfram.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEMZnpcHivWsIyuOyfpvEAHARIappX9swzgQ7QK1scBumsg4z9khPpq0VhDaftampze-GUQyrX_S6IRaIUAsE-Tkrl5C24smPEl_g72t6qJ7runRrRvhbNuFcWg2qPHx6jdReU9gwbmm5HgnXBJCA==)\n[5] [uz.zgora.pl](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGzmw1L0O1OwIGFaPfUPDPFrgatUYx0FsTMFdqewMJUpCv3RdfCOoEp8dgPUXK36tfj-AX63Og-otlvea7N0hB2fOft4-9e-O_3jU8HoFdrn_06VaJq_X4yr_PfPjAGcG5ZxR2XEYBvzuQokA==)\n[6] [wikipedia.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHzsrV15N_I4fHWfxZGFH1MNVka3JpSmToZbE7p_VC_N5Ictnhd7XK5erIciwpUa0D4CfCB57teBuq9_o-dvscvfRzQI-PkpiPKKCbk4OTmpX5z6