{"step_index":55,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:56:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:56:35+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:56:38+02:00\nThe search for \"\"gamma_t(G) \\le \\gamma_R(G)\" OR \"total domination number\" \"roman domination number\" \"less than or equal\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe relationship between the total domination number, denoted as $\\gamma_t(G)$, and the Roman domination number, denoted as $\\gamma_R(G)$, is a subject of study in graph theory[1][2][3].\n\nWhile these parameters are related as graph invariants[1][2], the inequality $\\gamma_t(G) \\le \\gamma_R(G)$ is **not a universal property** for all graphs.\n\n### Key Context\n*   **Definitions:**\n    *   **Total Domination Number ($\\gamma_t(G)$):** The minimum cardinality of a set of vertices $S$ such that every vertex in the graph is adjacent to at least one vertex in $S$[4].\n    *   **Roman Domination Number ($\\gamma_R(G)$):** The minimum weight of a function $f: V(G) \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$ such that every vertex with $f(v)=0$ is adjacent to at least one vertex $u$ with $f(u)=2$[5][6].\n*   **Comparison:** Researchers often compare these parameters, along with others like the total Roman domination number ($\\gamma_{tR}(G)$) and the standard domination number ($\\gamma(G)$), to understand the structural properties of different graph classes (e.g., trees, cubic graphs)[7][1][8][9].\n*   **Research Focus:** In the literature, studies frequently establish bounds and relationships between these invariants[1][2]. Because $\\gamma_t(G)$ requires the dominating set to induce a subgraph with no isolated vertices (total domination)[10][4], while $\\gamma_R(G)$ uses a weight-based function to satisfy domination requirements[5][6], their values can vary significantly depending on the graph's structure[8][9].\n\nIf you are looking for a specific inequality, it is often necessary to restrict the graph to a particular class (such as trees, bipartite graphs, or graphs with a minimum degree condition) to establish a firm relationship[7][8][11].\n\nSources:\n[1] [bg.ac.rs](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGJGE1KjVQdVe_4sMEwnzN1aF52rJyA1ZgIT-ESoc1tBScOaRVnublRqWcoVGR8dpk4y_ROGQzXw1sDRcHSq-Q4IWRTAe5TgYj4_KL8dvBg-Fn9KfoykuYaOAVPUc_ODXtpIXJljnng)\n[2] [mdpi.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHQy2uZsUoDkDLKKV7bjjxpXOQgv2YhPFXM4JFV_f-KDQ-9Z534VkOzL32IqfnGErGI1aNGN6L46VEuK_bGwlDDZnNeFx1ULRtlOSQoHR2MrG-bCMqMzbldkVihrv_G)\n[3] [ijsdr.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGuCYOLq7vkHg4hxV9FUnoVxICDtfId8PQlSGE-C3uwSt0Bauf62kQEeyzLyRisk01AK1h134td1dfiZUU4BIlcpqtiNea-B5rJ0kwzxQY3mBbjBQKUFbPeiP5zvC5bOaio1oA=)\n[4] [wolfram.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHI-IqCl7F27PlUROCN7R2CqomnSPGs2ypwc7eFi-a_WYhQ3sILrI1u2M-6_cYSwJ0rVfvUuy_EMJt0f5dmb9hDJg_6YG4ClnrL3s4jHabT9tdh-R_vl_uL8tS5HVLtYoQuiUfAxS_0WSU=)\n[5] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHvpIhQFj6c-DriXvZgFUM77zGmfDoWqgw_AATBY0NYtLBTmD-BI_6Eve7sB0WCTOEz49jgEtC2QURU6Iq7ZEVJuRUKosRZHRG-uitLvuaq7cs3DjLtwFQFZA==)\n[6] [emergentmind.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGNUfNzYAsUGFD5slZhVD0FuCv3X5LIT7L3joXY73rzB0q1dDUAYgrvfGyqiUuVhT7Idq8r9mUFNulytK3HdqCcYhgPNKhmH_LNmh1Nnf2sSCA3rqJWKIgs4tNxtr7xQV-vIkHWX0DukYBDyqdskgJ7tw==)\n[7] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEH3VraZE63Tv-PKOIk7FxLyiUZlhYg6zRm3-zYBjLKo7ZrOtd09cHCEsA9j5tVenIQrdAG8DxpjwBb5aeycHHFBuBGJ9ozvd1FiG8pYngHkTe0ZnIHk5FAEA==)\n[8] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF_2Cy8t24yNJMUbzVVfevocZJT9cit0bM5-YxcqkOrsxcQlnMyndlAIDdZ0M2_rGginHMU0oRXEb7IDLSHGAKK6p3v0xEFkYxuW70dvlce1p4FCQ0C4W2ufDl6r58a4dEvEIfilRtWm7hgLhDH7P5UjOstqawIhUyP7lHGwvqs6UlTOIYPDNaOLeOVvvwT6y4Pcj7uACd_dQT5GbM6L-xqA-lgs7