{"step_index":66,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:57:24Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:57:24+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:57:27+02:00\nThe search for \"\"total domination\" \"roman domination\" \"\\gamma_t\" \"\\gamma_R\" inequality\" returned the following summary:\nIn graph theory, the relationship between the **total domination number** $\\gamma_t(G)$ and the **total Roman domination number** $\\gamma_{tR}(G)$ (often denoted as $\\gamma_t^R(G)$) is a subject of study within domination theory[1][2].\n\n### Key Concepts\n*   **Total Domination Number $\\gamma_t(G)$**: The minimum cardinality of a set $D \\subseteq V(G)$ such that every vertex in $V(G)$ is adjacent to at least one vertex in $D$[3][1].\n*   **Total Roman Domination Number $\\gamma_{tR}(G)$**: The minimum weight of a function $f: V(G) \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$ such that every vertex with $f(v) = 0$ is adjacent to a vertex $u$ with $f(u) = 2$, and the set of vertices with $f(v) > 0$ induces a subgraph with no isolated vertices[2][4].\n\n### Inequalities and Relationships\nResearch into these parameters often focuses on bounding $\\gamma_{tR}(G)$ in terms of other invariants like the domination number $\\gamma(G)$ or the total domination number $\\gamma_t(G)$[5][4].\n\n*   **Comparison to Total Domination**: A known inequality in literature is:\n    $$\\gamma_{tR}(G) \\leq 2\\gamma_t(G)$$\n    This inequality has been established in studies regarding Roman domination variants, such as in the context of strongly chordal graphs[6].\n\n*   **Comparison to Classical Domination**: It is also common to relate these to the classical domination number $\\gamma(G)$. For instance, bounds such as $2\\gamma(G) \\leq \\gamma_{tR}(G) \\leq 3\\gamma(G)$ have been explored and improved upon in various papers[4].\n\n### Summary of Context\nThe parameters $\\gamma_t(G)$ and $\\gamma_{tR}(G)$ are distinct because the latter imposes an additional \"total\" constraint on the set of vertices with positive weights (they must induce a subgraph with no isolated vertices), making it a more restrictive and computationally complex parameter than standard Roman domination ($\\gamma_R(G)$)[1][2][4]. Research in this area frequently seeks to characterize graphs that achieve equality in these bounds or to provide sharp bounds for specific graph families[7][4].\n\nSources:\n[1] [isikun.edu.tr](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGuA9Bpwtyzrj9woOVDZTE26Vl5YKNEEIc2hiaLX2rusHk4I2OfhNEl3gbobRyn3EJ2qHSqV9vsY7Ki6JpNz-hHBRADcoctMhOYLCGmarCNHfA3WvzOFgMGxR7Mau9uJNoIkrkuC_y7aKuBnU8mMOxSt2ISmM0F)\n[2] [bg.ac.rs](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH8hl_KAG74_UfD7DTyrm_wLbuZou66Zsw-wUNTGUUANPyfCxx6r_EYDTuGHsOEnd2x1CCjez3brl72c9uOhXLfEpV0dmo9V1Xsva_LvMHXa-KhCABuO1cnPIBdTH-fguSMxcjEhl8=)\n[3] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHVtKGFj1YaKDbXM38Nst67kvMVFZqHQnRGvz3i0CFKRjirmDbEpruMf5XV6cOwsdMbDOLIhRrOwLX4HbGR4jH5LYtqyOv9FDEprN5O49VD_iX9QsRS1MaFyLCuVLsuW201DPJiWCn6qAsblzMjre5EF2eX1YTM96Nwlc_iNiYHOlsxmydj_f5GcXeyiI054Ky2-e6VxnDr3cENB9TO_HCJ42I-q0lQa6xrIqY20270RLHL4Hg=)\n[4] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEKmhEzBGgCMxauVS2Uhw_eONnXMv5WOXJdDL1n-Ag5aoWOq-effTeGXV2RdFDHUa6s-w9_U4vcb5nO4x7Z0OKR5qz3yAdOmW83ytOr0yb9kNILYeQjaNKIOmgXaMwim17Yj_yZhJfNbCovQKde-ao25W0rNQ0TNOy1XQYwGPnM-pXhi4ZHKv2RlFVMzcl5YSsdfn2QcjTBgwLbK6eeYdU=)\n[5] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFnTUEeXBnPS8yQhrhnkLeMGt5y9ZH8_W6gUpQmMxuXFk94x8CT-oM0_EtE8xJQ-Bspaf08Jy99nH6Tphy4aCtB4aytZu5CrSz4-K74eWuwLAQSVhaRRJBUUXxdYQt10fnq4VAt2QaWtHDBbgtYnB08EkYJF6qEssC5uodIaSMf-LK5pWN3CuLgVaK-thJD7-MPTJJ6gCEswJiHt4eTEn184nieymLnc5gXNdqJ4PcZD7bAqyvL19O1Oyo=)\n[6] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEb2TtTv82aGlIjG-HVcnLXVA143V57w2p5Uq2wxo2OkCfFb882KsWR_QOzJ70yCCudBsS9O6X9km8JVvuhpXTixeqARS48SZAhMmqqXrgdMkRa5QdSWyvG4T6f-vIe37deK9BoIXiMo3AThlEF0QbK3sJGk9FxluG9QFZ-ONmdHBXdjgNCcJehxVK-MSY6rOdsN5U=)\n[7] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHDJ2-T-MUO-LKzNghflPOEMNdj16mKbS6hyML3GgFz4Gn38cqx52rZDZWXJGEV-GzI98aOFWQ6ML1JW1Cmud5WFgjjeUiSNv_WecwF5ELARrp6g30m0wE2-vLrBUa5qLVjDdj6Sh6rHAgxDgTErwtGkxb3rvO1Pg31olxLWcArLJLnexwvbNMb37KneVZgTu8Te_CHtW9IIQ==)"}
